.TH TODOS 1.SH NAMEtodos, toos9 \- convert line ends between OS-9 and DOS.SH SYNOPSIS\fBtodos\fRfile ....br\fBtoos9\fR[\-1] file ....SH DESCRIPTION\fBtodos\fRturns OS-9 text into DOS text, every carriage return into a carriagereturn and line feed;\fBtoos9\fRdoes the reverse.  DESIGNA VLT, UTIL 2.80..PPBoth write the converted text into\fBtodos.___.\fIn\fRor\fBtoos9.___.\fIn\fRin the current directory, and then fork OS-9's own\fBdel\fRand\fBrename\fRto put it in place of the original.  Those come with your system; wherethey are missing the converted file is left under its temporary name,and you rename it yourself.  Stand in the file's directory and give abare name, since OS-9's\fBrename\fRwants a name rather than a pathlist..PP\fBautolf \-c \-C \-L\fRdoes the same conversions as a filter and renames nothing..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-1\fR\fR(\fBtoos9\fR)\fRconvert the German characters of the DOS code page -- a-umlaut and therest -- into\fBae\fRand so on..SH NOTESBoth need Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOautolf